{"id": 2107, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" id=\"vignettedescriptor\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">T</mark>his item has associated media that may require the use of headphones.\u00a0 Please ensure your system/speaker volume is set to an audible level.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">A 65-year-old man with a long history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the office for a routine checkup.\u00a0 The patient is a former smoker with a 15-pack-year history.\u00a0 He jogs 3 miles per day.\u00a0 BMI is 28.5 kg/m<sup style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">2</sup>.\u00a0 Family history is unremarkable.\u00a0 Auscultation findings over the cardiac apex are given below.\u00a0 The auscultation findings are best explained by which of the following?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Aortic root dilation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Atrial septal defect"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Left ventricular hypertrophy"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Mitral valve prolapse"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Pericardial effusion"}], "correctAnswer": "C", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_t58817\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">E</mark>xtra (gallop) heart sounds</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Features</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Associated disorders*</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">S3</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Heard just after S2</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Caused by abrupt cessation of LV filling during early diastole</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Generally indicative of elevated LV filling pressure</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Heart failure</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">High-output states (eg,\u00a0thyrotoxicosis)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mitral or aortic regurgitation</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">S4</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Heard just before S1</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Caused by blood striking a stiff LV wall during atrial contraction</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Concentric LV hypertrophy</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Restrictive cardiomyopathy</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Acute myocardial infarction</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">*S3 can be normal in children, young adults &amp; pregnant patients.\u00a0 A soft S4 can be normal in older adults (eg, age &gt;70).</p><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">LV</strong> = left ventricular.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient has a <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">low pitch</strong>, extra <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-enznejln3917oqgvvhdff4bd\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-enznejln3917oqgvvhdff4bd\">heart sound</a> at the <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">end of diastole</strong> (just before S1) known as an <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">S4</strong>.\u00a0 An S4 is thought to be caused by blood striking a stiff ventricular wall during atrial contraction.\u00a0 It can be a normal finding in elderly patients (eg, age &gt;70) due to a physiologic, age-related decrease in left ventricular (LV) compliance.\u00a0 However, in younger patients it typically indicates pathologic LV wall stiffening, most commonly due to <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-b7sn6q878a91etuxn9nfbnv8\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-b7sn6q878a91etuxn9nfbnv8\">concentric</a> LV hypertrophy in response to prolonged hypertension (likely the cause in this case).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">In comparison, an <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-ptvtynx86fo5c4ct2i6ans6e\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-ptvtynx86fo5c4ct2i6ans6e\">S3</a> is heard in early diastole (just after S2) and is thought to result from reverberation of sound due to blood filling an enlarged and volume-overloaded ventricle (as occurs with eccentric hypertrophy in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction).\u00a0 Both S3 and S4 are best heard at the cardiac apex with the bell of the stethoscope.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Aortic root dilation can cause <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-zfpq5g09mav0admrlo602py8\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-zfpq5g09mav0admrlo602py8\">aortic regurgitation</a>, which is recognized by a decrescendo early diastolic murmur.\u00a0 Aortic regurgitation resulting from aortic root (as opposed to valvular) pathology is typically <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-o3rgyenl45o54aqufibluoxq\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-o3rgyenl45o54aqufibluoxq\">best heard</a> at the right upper sternal border.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice B)</strong>\u00a0 An <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-twhdt0wo85knp4dlhrtbwsr0\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-twhdt0wo85knp4dlhrtbwsr0\">atrial septal defect</a> typically causes a faint, crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur (best heard at the left upper sternal border) caused by increased blood flow through the pulmonic valve (ie, pulmonic flow murmur).\u00a0 Splitting of S2 is wide and fixed.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-sb86inrg2q6ql4oaaupgho2d\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-sb86inrg2q6ql4oaaupgho2d\">Mitral valve prolapse</a> classically causes a midsystolic click followed by the late systolic murmur of mitral regurgitation (best heard at the cardiac apex).\u00a0 The click is relatively high pitched, and the <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-c31qitbkbiy6zt85pfg4utfv\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-c31qitbkbiy6zt85pfg4utfv\">timing</a> varies with changes in LV blood volume.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Pericardial effusion commonly occurs as a complication of acute pericarditis, which may be recognized by a <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-j3nfymk62yaofije5ydfrcp4\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-j3nfymk62yaofije5ydfrcp4\">triphasic pericardial friction rub</a> best heard at the left lower sternal border with the patient leaning forward.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>An S4 is a low frequency, extra heart sound that occurs at the end of diastole (just before S1).\u00a0 It is caused by blood striking a stiff ventricular wall during atrial contraction; it can be a normal finding in elderly patients (eg, age &gt;70), but typically indicates pathologic ventricular wall stiffening in younger patients (most commonly due to concentric left ventricular hypertrophy in response to chronic hypertension).\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "An S4 is a low frequency, extra heart sound that occurs at the end of diastole (just before S1).  It is caused by blood striking a stiff ventricular wall during atrial contraction; it can be a normal finding in elderly patients (eg, age >70), but typically indicates pathologic ventricular wall stiffening in younger patients (most commonly due to concentric left ventricular hypertrophy in response to chronic hypertension).\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u2